diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end IP reachability and BGP between our site (195.11.14.0/24 via router1) and the partner (200.1.1.0/24 via router2). The network is healthy: topology-wide pings succeed both ways with 0% loss (pc1→pc2 avg ~2.2 ms; pc2→pc1 avg ~1.1 ms). On router1 and router2 the eBGP session over 193.10.11.0/24 is Established, each side is receiving exactly one prefix from the other (router1 PfxRcd=1 for 200.1.1.0/24; router2 PfxRcd=1 for 195.11.14.0/24), and the learned routes are installed in the RIB and kernel (router1 shows B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2; router2 shows B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1). All interfaces (router1 eth0/eth1, router2 eth0/eth1, pc1/pc2 eth0) are up with correct addressing and the hosts have proper default routes.

Mechanism: If the partner had truly “fallen off” due to a routing issue, we would see the BGP session down and/or the partner prefix missing from the routing table, causing pings to fail. None of these symptoms are present.

Localization: No faulty subsystem found; FRR BGP, kernel routes, and L2 links on router1 and router2 are operating normally.

Root cause: Not reproducible in the network—no routing or reachability fault observed.

Corrective fix: No network change required. If the user still sees issues, focus on application/DNS or the partner service endpoint rather than routing (e.g., verify service ports or names rather than IP reachability). 
